NNAAMMEE
     ccrroonnttaabb — maintain crontab files for individual users

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ccrroonnttaabb [--uu _u_s_e_r] _f_i_l_e
     ccrroonnttaabb [--ee | --ll | --rr] [--uu _u_s_e_r]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ccrroonnttaabb is the program used to install, deinstall, or list the tables
     used to drive the cron(8) daemon.  Each user can have their own
     crontab(5), and though these are files in _/_v_a_r_/_c_r_o_n_/_t_a_b_s, they are not
     intended to be edited directly.

     The first form of this command is used to install a new crontab from some
     named file, or standard input if the pseudo-filename ‘-’ is given.

     If the _/_v_a_r_/_c_r_o_n_/_c_r_o_n_._a_l_l_o_w file exists, then you must be listed therein
     in order to use ccrroonnttaabb.  If the _/_v_a_r_/_c_r_o_n_/_c_r_o_n_._a_l_l_o_w file does not exist
     but the _/_v_a_r_/_c_r_o_n_/_c_r_o_n_._d_e_n_y file does exist, then you must _n_o_t be listed
     in the _/_v_a_r_/_c_r_o_n_/_c_r_o_n_._d_e_n_y file in order to use ccrroonnttaabb.  If neither of
     these files exists then only the super user will be allowed to use
     ccrroonnttaabb.  _N_O_T_E: if they exist, _/_v_a_r_/_c_r_o_n_/_c_r_o_n_._a_l_l_o_w and
     _/_v_a_r_/_c_r_o_n_/_c_r_o_n_._d_e_n_y must be readable by group crontab.  If ccrroonnttaabb is un‐
     able to read the files, users will not be allowed to use ccrroonnttaabb.

     The options are as follows:

     --ee        Edit the current crontab using the editor specified by the
               VISUAL or EDITOR environment variables.  After you exit from
               the editor, the modified crontab(5) will be installed automati‐
               cally.

     --ll        Causes the current crontab to be displayed on standard output.

     --rr        Causes the current crontab to be removed.

     --uu _u_s_e_r   Specifies the name of the user whose crontab(5) is to be
               edited.  If this option is not given, ccrroonnttaabb examines “your”
               crontab(5); i.e., the crontab of the person executing the com‐
               mand.  Note that su(1) can confuse ccrroonnttaabb and that if you are
               running inside of su(1) you should always use the --uu option for
               safety's sake.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     EDITOR, VISUAL
             Specifies an editor to use.  If both EDITOR and VISUAL are set,
             VISUAL takes precedence.  If neither EDITOR nor VISUAL are set,
             the default is vi(1).

FFIILLEESS
     /var/cron/cron.allow  list of users allowed to use crontab
     /var/cron/cron.deny   list of users prohibited from using crontab
     /var/cron/tabs        directory of individual crontabs

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     at(1), crontab(5), cron(8)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The ccrroonnttaabb utility is compliant with the IEEE Std 1003.1-2008
     (“POSIX.1”) specification.

     The flag [--uu] is an extension to that specification.

     The flag [--ee] is marked by IEEE Std 1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”) as being op‐
     tional.

     The cron.allow/deny mechanism is marked by IEEE Std 1003.1-2008
     (“POSIX.1”) as being an X/Open System Interfaces option.

